---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../site';

const rows = [
  { dimension: 'Gateway port', netherlands: 'Rotterdam — Europe\u2019s largest', germany: 'Hamburg — Germany\u2019s largest' },
  { dimension: 'VAT rate', netherlands: '21% (deferrable under Article 23)', germany: '19% (no full deferral scheme)' },
  { dimension: 'China rail terminus', netherlands: 'Limited', germany: 'Major (Hamburg, Duisburg)' },
  { dimension: 'Hinterland reach', netherlands: 'Rhine barge to Germany, France, Switzerland', germany: 'Central Europe by rail' },
  { dimension: 'Best fit', netherlands: 'EU-wide distribution hub, inventory holding', germany: 'German market + Central Europe, rail-split' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Should I set up my EU distribution base in the Netherlands or Germany?',
    a: 'For EU-wide distribution and inventory holding, the Netherlands — its Article 23 VAT-deferral licence lets you hold China inventory without paying import VAT until goods enter a member state. For serving the German market itself or a sea-rail split, Germany is stronger.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which has the lower VAT, the Netherlands or Germany?',
    a: 'Germany, at 19% versus the Netherlands\u2019 21%. But the Netherlands\u2019 Article 23 deferral can defer the VAT cash outflow entirely for inventory held in bonded storage, which often outweighs the two-point rate difference.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does China rail freight serve the Netherlands?',
    a: 'Less than Germany. The New Silk Road\u2019s main European termini are in Germany (Hamburg, Duisburg) and Poland (Małaszewicze, Łódź). Rotterdam is primarily a deep-sea gateway, though it is building rail capacity.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I distribute to Germany from a Rotterdam base?',
    a: 'Yes — Rotterdam\u2019s Rhine barge network reaches the German Ruhr and beyond cheaply. Many EU distributors run a Dutch base and barge German-bound cargo inland rather than running a separate German operation.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Netherlands vs Germany for China Imports — Distribution Base"
  description="Netherlands versus Germany as a China-to-Europe import and distribution base: Rotterdam vs Hamburg, VAT deferral, rail access and hinterland reach."
  canonical={`${SITE_URL}/europe/netherlands-vs-germany/`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Netherlands vs Germany' },
        ]}
      />
      <h1>Netherlands vs Germany for China imports</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The two heavyweight entry points for China cargo are not interchangeable —
        one is a distribution machine, the other a market plus a rail terminus.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <blockquote class="tldr">
      <p>
        <strong>TL;DR:</strong> The Netherlands is the better base for EU-wide
        distribution and inventory holding, thanks to Rotterdam\u2019s Rhine barge
        network and Article 23 VAT deferral. Germany is the better base for
        serving the German market directly or running a sea-rail split, with
        lower VAT and the New Silk Road terminus at Hamburg and Duisburg.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'dimension', label: 'Dimension' },
        { key: 'netherlands', label: 'Netherlands' },
        { key: 'germany', label: 'Germany' },
      ]}
      rows={rows}
      caption="Indicative comparison of the two leading China-to-Europe entry points."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The VAT deferral that tips the scale</h2>
    <p>
      The single biggest structural difference is not the two-point VAT gap
      (Germany 19% versus Netherlands 21%) — it is the Netherlands\u2019 Article 23
      licence. It lets an importer hold China inventory in bonded storage at
      Rotterdam and account for import VAT only when goods actually enter a
      member state. For a distributor holding stock or re-exporting across the
      EU, that defers a large, recurring cash outflow that Germany\u2019s system
      does not offer in the same form.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why "cheapest VAT" is the wrong way to pick a base. Germany's 19%
      looks better on paper, but a Dutch deferral can remove the VAT from your
      cash flow entirely until the sale. Price the cash-flow effect, not the
      headline rate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The rail split is Germany\u2019s edge</h2>
    <p>
      Germany is where deep-sea and New Silk Road rail physically meet. Hamburg
      takes both ocean vessels and China trains, and Duisburg is the largest
      inland rail terminal in Europe. That makes Germany the natural home for a
      sea-rail split — part of your volume by ocean, part by rail — which the
      Netherlands cannot match on the rail side. Rotterdam is building rail
      capacity, but its identity remains deep-sea.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>They overlap more than they compete</h2>
    <p>
      The practical truth is that many importers use both, not either. A Dutch
      base at Rotterdam with Rhine barge distribution into the German Ruhr is a
      common, cost-effective pattern — you get Dutch VAT deferral and still
      reach German buyers by water. The clean "Netherlands or Germany" framing
      is usually a false choice; the real question is whether your priority is
      EU-wide distribution (lean Dutch) or the German market plus a rail option
      (lean German).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <Cta />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
